  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. Swings grow when learning rate > 0.33 and diverge above 0.67.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b, so it is exactly zero at 64. One step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows use scale 1. Each one's length equals its real step, so the orange tip is where the ball lands next. The grey arrow is −learning rate × true slope.",
    "Diverged: the loss passes 6, the loss at the bowl's steep edge. The run stops there and the ball is drawn at the edge, but the readout keeps the true loss. Finished: 40 steps.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence. Each new run uses the next noise values. The trail and ghost hold up to 41 points: the start plus 40 steps."
  ]
}